00:09:55.228 --> 00:10:02.548
A Canadian bank, quote, near $159.59, with a 15x earnings multiple, can look cheap until

00:10:02.548 --> 00:10:06.288
you ask whether the bank actually earns more than it costs to fund the equity.

00:10:06.288 --> 00:10:08.788
C.I.B.C. is the worked example.

00:10:09.428 --> 00:10:13.388
Start from book value of about 72.14 a share in Canadian funds.

00:10:13.848 --> 00:10:17.668
Apply average return on equity of 16.76%.

00:10:17.668 --> 00:10:23.648
Subtract a cost of equity near 5.8 a share, and you are left with roughly 6.65 of excess

00:10:23.648 --> 00:10:25.268
return per share to capitalize.

00:10:25.928 --> 00:10:31.148
That capitalized excess is the estimate of intrinsic value, and in this run it sits above the

00:10:31.148 --> 00:10:37.168
current quote. A stable book of $69.96 a share anchors the terminal value so near-term earnings

00:10:37.168 --> 00:10:42.548
noise does not yank the whole model around. Think of a basement suite that throws off more cash

00:10:42.548 --> 00:10:47.628
each year than the carrying cost of the house. That extra cash is why you would pay more than the

00:10:47.628 --> 00:10:54.288
assessed value of the walls. An excess return math is just that extra turned into a price. Professional

00:10:54.288 --> 00:10:59.868
desks refresh this on Bloomberg or fact-set every day. You can approximate it in a spreadsheet

00:10:59.868 --> 00:11:05.928
with consensus ROE and stable EPS from Yahoo Finance or simply Wall Street, then

00:11:05.928 --> 00:11:07.808
compare implied value to the market.

00:11:08.348 --> 00:11:12.868
The habit that matters is rerunning the sheet after every quarter so you can see whether

00:11:12.868 --> 00:11:15.808
the excess return margin is widening or narrowing.

00:11:16.468 --> 00:11:20.668
The misconception is treating a low PE as undervaluation by itself.

00:11:21.188 --> 00:11:25.728
The fix is to check that return on equity actually clears the cost of capital before

00:11:25.728 --> 00:11:27.488
you call the discount an opportunity.

00:11:27.488 --> 00:11:33.048
Open your brokerage, pull the options chain on one U.S. listed name you already follow,

00:11:33.368 --> 00:11:38.588
take the 30-day at the money covered call, and convert that premium into an annualized

00:11:38.588 --> 00:11:40.608
yield on the capital the shares tie up.

00:11:41.088 --> 00:11:44.108
That is how you finish the structure albumaril only sketched.

